# AMD64 thunk export spec -- vcruntime140_1.dll.
#
# Added from the 2026-08-15 first-real-game triage: with the application's own
# shipped DLLs now loadable (find_dll_file() searches the load path for the
# guest's machine instead of refusing it outright), what stopped
# quake2ex_steam.exe was Wine builtins with no guest thunk -- each reported as
# c000007b, the loader correctly refusing to splice a native ppc64 module into
# a guest call.  Nothing here is hand-written: the export list comes from this
# module's own .spec.
DLL vcruntime140_1.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-08-29, guest-cxx-eh-plan Session A: confirmed empty, and why it stays
# that way for now
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This module's .spec has exactly one non-stub entry, `__CxxFrameHandler4`
# (dlls/vcruntime140_1/vcruntime140_1.spec:1), and it is declared in NO Wine
# header the oracle reads.  FROM-SPEC auto therefore refuses it ("no
# declaration found") the same as any other undeclared name -- confirmed by
# building this module and reading spec2thunk's own "WARNING: every export
# was refused; emitting a module with no [stub] exports" line, and by
# spec2thunk-check, which passes an all-refused module rather than treating
# it as a build failure (see the tool's own comment: "an all-refused module
# is still worth emitting").  So: yes, this thunk really exports nothing
# today, and both games' VCRUNTIME140_1.__CxxFrameHandler4 import binds to
# ntdll's missing-import sentinel when THIS module answers the load.
#
# It does NOT need fixing here.  __CxxFrameHandler4 is not an API a guest
# calls; it is the LANGUAGE PERSONALITY ROUTINE the unwinder invokes with
# that image's exception/context records and expects to walk that image's
# FuncInfo4 tables and resume in its code -- exactly the __CxxFrameHandler3
# class vcruntime140.thunks refuses by name, for the same reason.  The
# eventual fix, once guest-cxx-eh-plan.md's Session B lands real x86-64
# FH4 code in dlls/guestcrt (compiling Wine's own handler4.c +
# except_x86_64.c, per that plan's section 4), is exactly the setjmp/
# _CxxThrowException shape: `FORWARD __CxxFrameHandler4
# guestcrt.__CxxFrameHandler4`.  Adding that FORWARD now, ahead of the guest
# code it would point at, would trade a diagnosable "no such export" hole for
# a load-time forwarder-resolution failure against a symbol that does not
# exist yet -- worse, not better.  Session A's actual unblock for these two
# titles is elsewhere: Proton already stages a REAL x86-64
# vcruntime140_1.dll (Wine's own build, genuine FH4 machine code) into every
# Proton prefix, and THAT module's own imports -- not this one's exports --
# are what vcruntime140.thunks and ucrtbase.thunks now serve
# (_CxxThrowException, __C_specific_handler, __processing_throw).  A non-
# Proton prefix, or a builtin-DLL override that prefers this module, still
# gets the named refusal above, honestly, until Session B exists.
